---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import { SITE } from "../config/site.mjs";
---

<Layout
	title="About Veena — Independent, On-Device Speech Recognition for India"
	description="Veena is a free speech to text app for all 22 scheduled languages of India, built by an independent developer to run on your device instead of in the cloud."
	canonicalURL={`${SITE.url}/about/`}
>
	<Header />
	<main id="main-content" role="main">
		<section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-20">
			<h1 class="text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-8">About Veena</h1>

			<div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-600">
				<p class="text-xl mb-8">
					Veena is a speech to text application built for India's languages. It
					turns spoken words into written text in all 22 scheduled languages of
					the Indian Constitution — from Telugu, Hindi, Tamil, Bengali and
					Marathi to Bodo, Santali, Konkani and Sanskrit.
				</p>

				<p class="mb-6">
					Speech recognition for Indian languages has historically meant sending
					your voice to a cloud server. That is a poor fit for sensitive
					conversations, for people with unreliable internet, and for languages
					that big cloud providers ignore. Veena takes the opposite approach:
					everything runs on your own device.
				</p>

				<p class="mb-12">
					Tap the microphone, speak in your language, and Veena converts your
					words into editable text you can copy or share. The app downloads a
					speech model once during setup; after that it works completely
					offline.
				</p>

				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
					Built Telugu first, open to all 22 languages
				</h2>
				<p class="mb-6">
					Veena started as a Telugu speech to text app — that is still how the
					mobile stores describe it. Telugu gets its own dedicated recognition
					model, tuned for the language. Every other scheduled language is
					covered by one multilingual model, built on IndicConformer from
					AI4Bharat, where each language decodes through its own vocabulary so a
					sentence in Kannada does not drift into Marathi.
				</p>

				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">Private by design</h2>
				<p class="mb-12">
					No account. No cloud transcription. No telemetry. Your recordings and
					transcripts are stored locally and never uploaded anywhere. The mobile
					apps ask for microphone permission, plus network access for that first
					model download, and nothing else. The full story is on the <a
						href="/privacy"
						class="text-blue-600 hover:underline">privacy page</a
					>.
				</p>

				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
					On the devices you own
				</h2>
				<p class="mb-6">
					Veena is available today for Android and iPhone, and as a Windows
					desktop app that types into whatever you are working in. Desktop
					builds for macOS and Linux are in development. Whatever the device,
					your speech stays on it.
				</p>

				<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6">
					Made by an independent developer
				</h2>
				<p class="mb-12">
					Veena is developed by Nikhil Reddy Avuthu. It is a small, independent
					project — feedback, feature requests, and bug reports are welcome at
					<a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`} class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
						>{SITE.email}</a
					>.
				</p>
			</div>
		</section>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>
